    "heading level" : 1,
    "font" : "Times New Roman,Bold",
    "font size" : 11.04,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Face Validity  "
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 62,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.333, 146.253, 286.667, 232.92 ],
    "font" : null,
    "font size" : 12.0,
    "content" : "Most of the participants in both groups were female (19 females VS. 11 males) with ethnicity (14 Malay vs. 7 Chinese and 9 Indian). Although the participants did not live with their parents, 40.2% of them had at least one living parent. Malay Paired Samples t-Test, the results showed that the mean score of 6 coping methods including active coping (t=-5.85, P<0.001), of emotional supports(t =-4.80, P<0.001), of reframing(t=-3.89, P<0.001 ), planning(t=-4.91, P<0.001) and acceptance (t=-5.98, P<0.001) methods significantly increased after interventional sessions. Meanwhile, the mean score of 3 coping methods including selfdenial   (t 2.80, P<0.02) and self-blame (t=7.24, P 0.001) significantly decreased in post-test. No significant change was found in mean score of substance use, behavior disengagement, venting; humor and religion (p>0.05). Using using using"
  } ]
